<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wanderer above the Sea of Fog</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1818</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Wanderer über dem Nebelmeer</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wanderer above the Sea of Fog is a painting of about 1818 by the German Romantic artist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspar-david-friedrich">Caspar David Friedrich</a>. A man seen from behind stands on a rocky precipice above a sea of fog pierced by ridges, trees and mountains, a landscape Friedrich composed in the studio from sketches of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> Sandstone Mountains. It has been interpreted as an emblem of self-reflection, as an evocation of the sublime and as an expression of his German liberal and nationalist feeling. It has been displayed at the Hamburger Kunsthalle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> since 1970.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wanderer-above-the-sea-of-fog.jpg" alt="Wanderer above the Sea of Fog" width="320" height="407" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Caspar David Friedrich · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Friedrich,_Caspar_David_-_Wanderer_%C3%BCber_dem_Nebelmeer.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
